# Normalise line endings in the repository (GFP-98).
#
# Development happens on Windows with core.autocrlf=true and there was no
# .gitattributes, so a file authored with LF was committed and checked back out
# with CRLF. For db_script/ that was not cosmetic: grocery_planner/db.py hashes
# each script to detect a migration edited after it was applied, and the
# LF -> CRLF round trip changed the hash. The result was a hard refusal to open
# the database ("a previously-applied migration was edited after the fact"),
# triggered by nothing more than writing a migration and then committing it.
#
# db.py's checksum is now line-ending independent, which is the real fix. This
# file stops the churn at source as well, so the working tree matches what is
# committed and diffs stay clean.
* text=auto

# SQL schema scripts: always LF in the working tree. These are hashed, and are
# only ever read by Python, never by a Windows tool that needs CRLF.
*.ddl text eol=lf
*.dml text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one place CRLF genuinely matters -- older Windows
# PowerShell hosts can mis-parse an LF-only script.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary-ish assets git should not touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.xlsm binary
*.xlsx binary
